[THOUGHT] This is a routine 5-year well-child visit with no acute or chronic concerns reported. By design, preventive well-child visits at age 5 do not require a disease diagnosis — the clinical objective is screening, surveillance, immunization verification, and developmental/behavioral assessment. However, the case summary explicitly states that *critical standard-of-care elements are missing*:  
- No growth measurements (height/weight/BMI) — essential for identifying undernutrition, obesity, or growth disorders;  
- No vision or hearing screening — mandated by AAP/ACIP for kindergarten entry to detect amblyopia risk or undiagnosed hearing loss impacting learning;  
- No developmental screening (ASQ-3 not completed within past 3 months) — critical for identifying early signs of autism, language delay, or motor delays before school entry;  
- Immunization status unverified — leaving child vulnerable to vaccine-preventable diseases and potentially non-compliant with school entry requirements.  

These omissions represent failures to meet minimum evidence-based standards for a 5-year well-child visit (per AAP Bright Futures, CDC, and state school entry requirements). While “No diagnosis indicated” is *clinically appropriate* for an asymptomatic child, the *visit itself is incomplete* — not because a diagnosis is expected, but because required preventive services were not delivered or documented. Since this is Turn 10/10 (maximum reached), a forced decision is required. The absence of any diagnosis is acceptable *only if all mandated screenings and assessments have been performed and found normal*. Here, they are *not performed* — so the visit cannot be considered complete or clinically sufficient.

[DECISION] REJECT

[FEEDBACK] This visit cannot be closed as complete without: (1) documented height, weight, and BMI percentile plotted on CDC 2000 charts; (2) formal vision screening (HOTV or LEA symbols at 10 ft) with per-eye results; (3) documented hearing screening result (within last 12 months); (4) completed ASQ-3 (or equivalent validated tool) with score interpretation; and (5) verified immunization status with catch-up administered or scheduled. All are required prior to visit conclusion.

